// Structured concurrency helpers. Every callback is a child task owned by
// the caller's activation or session; retaining or discarding a callback
// result never detaches that child. Runtime bindings implement the scheduling
// and cancellation boundary while this pinned module fixes the portable API.
//
// Callback arrays use one common successful result type. `parallel` preserves
// input order in its result list even when children finish in another order;
// an empty callback array returns an empty list.
// Each child is a separate activation: a sibling failure does not undo writes
// already committed by another child. The first observed failure cancels
// unfinished siblings; after joining, the lowest input-index ordinary failure
// is primary and remaining failures are ordered causes.
pub fn parallel<T>(callbacks: [fn(): T]): [T] = std.concurrent.parallel(callbacks);

// Apply one transformation in an owned child activation per input. Results
// are joined in input order even when child completion order differs. An
// empty input starts no children and returns an empty list. Each transformed
// callback has the same transaction, failure and cancellation rules as
// `parallel` itself.
pub fn parallel_map<T, U>(values: [T], transform: fn(T): U): [U] =
    parallel(std.collection.map(values, value => () => transform(value)));

// Return the first observed successful result, then cancel and join every
// losing child. If all children fail, report the lowest input-index ordinary
// failure. Simultaneous successes are resolved by recorded completion order;
// no wall-clock order is promised. An empty callback array fails without
// scheduling work.
pub fn race<T>(callbacks: [fn(): T]): T = std.concurrent.race(callbacks);

// Run one zero-argument callback as an owned child. The duration is elapsed
// time: a negative value is an argument error. On expiry, request cancellation
// and join the child before raising the timeout diagnostic; no partial result
// is returned. Zero duration checks for an already-terminal result before it
// cancels unfinished work. A timeout does not detach or transfer the child.
pub fn timeout<T>(callback: fn(): T, duration: Duration): T =
    std.concurrent.timeout(callback, duration);

// Wait for a nonnegative elapsed duration and return null on success. Even a
// zero-duration wait requires an explicitly admitted clock provider; with one,
// it checks cancellation and returns without waiting. Waiting is
// cancellation-aware and has a clock/waiting effect, so this operation is
// unavailable to declaration assertions. The requested instant is a
// lower-bound target, not an exact scheduling guarantee.
pub fn sleep(duration: Duration): Null = std.concurrent.sleep(duration);
